              "value": {
                "kind": "scalar",
                "value": "!!<*yamlover: $defs: chapter>\nA Pinch of Math\n# A chapter mixing two ways of showing math: inline formulas in marklower prose ($$…$$),\n# and whole standalone LaTeX chunks. The chapter schema is attached via the tag; each LaTeX\n# chunk carries its own inline schema, !!<format: text/x-latex> — no meta overlay needed.\n# The body is positional: prose and LaTeX chunks in order (docs/documents/chapter).\n- |\n    Mathematics likes to hide **big ideas** in *short symbols*. The most famous of all,\n    Euler's identity $$e^{i\\pi} + 1 = 0$$, ties together five constants in a single breath —\n    note how $$e^{i\\pi}$$ sits *inside* this sentence. marklower also styles `inline code`,\n    __bold__, and ~~struck-out~~ text.\n  .yo: fragments:\n    mqgwoar6-xbn407: !!<*:: yamlover: $defs: fragment> \"Mathematics\"\n      &:: yamlover: ontos: colors: green: -\n      type: \"text\"\n      prefix: \"\"\n      suffix: \" likes to hide \"\n      created: 2026-06-16T17:18:11.059Z\n- |\n  When a formula deserves a line of its own, give it a whole chunk — see the Gaussian\n  integral at [:2](:2), a standalone LaTeX block (that link is relative to\n  this document; the title, being the node's self-value, consumes no index).\n- !!<format: text/x-latex> |\n  \\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} e^{-x^2}\\,dx = \\sqrt{\\pi}\n- |\n  Inline math also handles the everyday: the area of a circle is $$A = \\pi r^2$$ and the\n  quadratic formula $$x = \\frac{-b \\pm \\sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a}$$ both flow through the prose.\n- !!<format: text/x-latex> |\n  \\sum_{k=1}^{n} k = \\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\n",
